  "query_preview": "..\nTonight thru Thursday: As low pressure and an associated\nshortwave push inland, expect periods of rain across the Inland\nNW. One round of precip is forecast to increase through the day\nWednesday with a second Wednesday night into Thursday AM. The \nhighest precipitation potential and amounts are forecast around \nthe southeast WA and mountains zones, with around 0.25 to 0.50 \ninches through Thursday night, locally higher. Some embedded \nt-storms are also forecast, but they are not expected to be very\nstrong at this time. We will have to keep an eye on any burn \nscars that may see rain for possible flooding/debris flows, but \nconfidence is low. Winds will be breezy through the period, with\nthe strongest winds on Wednesday out of the south to southwest \ngusting between 20 to 40 mph. These strongest winds are forecast\nover central WA. Central WA will have less rain than other \nareas, so we will have to watch for some patchy blowing dust as \nwell. Gusty winds are forecast for Thursday too, with gusts \naround 15-30 mph. Temperatures will be notably cooler, with \nhighs in the mid-60s to upper 70s Wednesday and 60s to low 70s \nSaturday. Overnight lows will start to drop into the 30s and 40s\nin spots. We will have to keep an eye toward possible frost in \nsome areas over eastern WA and ID, in the sheltered valley and \noutlying areas, Thursday night into Friday AM. \n\nFriday to Monday: The Inland NW remains dominated by low \npressure, as models trend to the low being cut off from the jet \nover the weekend. While model agreement isn\u2019t terribly high yet,\nwe anticipate the showery and cool pattern to continue, with \noccasional embedded t-storm chances. /Solveig /KD",
